# Copy to ~/.omnilane/routing.local.yaml — lines here override routing.yaml.
# Same format: lane: vendor model effort   (first match per lane wins)

# Example: send triage to a cheaper local setup and disable the grok lane.
# triage:      codex gpt-6-luna low
# live-search: off - -

# consult is a multi-vendor direct-target chain. configure.sh intentionally
# skips it because that menu writes one candidate per lane. If overriding it,
# retain every vendor you want to address by name:
# consult: codex gpt-6-astra xhigh | claude claude-fable-5-1 xhigh | grok grok-4.7 high | gemini gemini-3.8-flash-medium -

# ── Starter profiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncomment ONE block that matches what you actually subscribe to.
# You never need every vendor — unmatched lanes fall back down the chain
# in routing.yaml automatically.

# Profile: Claude-only (no Codex/Gemini/Grok) — route everything to Claude.
# hardest-coding:  claude claude-fable-5-2 xhigh
# bulk-mechanical: claude claude-sonnet-5-5 high
# triage:          claude claude-haiku-4-5 -
# hard-judgment:   claude claude-fable-5-2 xhigh
# ui-draft:        claude claude-opus-5-5 high
# long-context:    claude claude-opus-5-5 xhigh
# fast-agentic:    claude claude-sonnet-5-5 medium
# live-search:     off - -
# coding-overflow: off - -

# Profile: Claude Code (Fable 5.1) as the main loop — let Fable keep judgment/taste,
# push only coding volume out to Codex.
# hard-judgment: claude claude-fable-5-1 xhigh
# taste-final: claude claude-fable-5-1 xhigh

# Profile: Opus 5.5 balanced controller / independent review (opt-in).
# Keep cross-vendor fallbacks explicit; this does not create a controller lane.
# hard-judgment: claude claude-opus-5-5 xhigh | codex gpt-6-astra max | grok grok-4.7 high
# taste-final: claude claude-opus-5-5 high | codex gpt-6-astra xhigh | grok grok-4.7 high | gemini gemini-3.8-flash-high -

# Profile: Codex-heavy (Sol main) — keep the hard lanes on Codex, Claude for taste.
# taste-final:     claude claude-sonnet-5-5 xhigh
# long-context:    codex gpt-6-astra medium

# Profile: no Codex subscription — promote Claude/Gemini into the coding lanes.
# hardest-coding:  claude claude-fable-5-2 xhigh
# bulk-mechanical: gemini gemini-3.8-flash-high -
# triage:          gemini gemini-3.8-flash-low -

# Profile: value — one effort notch or one model cheaper than the shipped first
# candidate. Scores are AA Intelligence Index v5.0.1 (proposed snapshot
# aa-v5.0.1-2026-11-10-v1); per-lane cost columns are in
# docs/reports/aa-v5.0.1-lanes-2026-11-10.txt. Take this when you pay per token
# and the work is not correctness-critical; the shipped defaults buy the points back.
# hardest-coding:  codex gpt-6.1-sol xhigh      # 52 pts vs Fable 5.2 xhigh 62
# hard-judgment:   claude claude-fable-5-2 medium # 56 pts vs xhigh 62
# taste-final:     claude claude-sonnet-5-5 high # 42 pts vs xhigh 50
# fast-agentic:    codex gpt-6.1-terra medium   # 38 pts vs GPT-6.1 Sol medium 46
# ui-draft:        claude claude-opus-5-5 medium # 48 pts vs high 52
# bulk-mechanical already leads with a value pick (GPT-6.1 Sol medium, 46 pts) — leave it.
#
# Cheaper still, and correspondingly riskier — DeepSeek is outside the AA score
# table here (figures from the 2026-08-02 leaderboard) and was a heavy
# hallucinator then (V4 Flash 0731 84%), so route only work whose output is
# mechanical rather than factual, and verify anything it asserts:
# triage:          deepseek deepseek-v4-flash - # 49.9 pts @ $0.03, ~60% under post-cut Luna (advise-only: cannot edit files)
